  "_commit": {
    "id": "d45d6acd79283483e20c92595a48c6a3bd155941",
    "author": "Ossama Edbali <ossedb@gmail.com>",
    "committer": "Ossama Edbali <ossedb@gmail.com>",
    "message": "feat(chrom-peak-rects): allow the peak rectangles to own a panel\n\n`lp_chrom_peak_rects()` previously errored unless `lp_mass_trace()` or\n`lp_intensity_map()` had been called first, so it could only decorate an\nexisting panel. It now also works on its own, drawing the rectangles on an\notherwise empty retention time / m/z frame with one facet per sample. That\ncovers the second of the two xcms methods sharing this primitive: the standalone\nform is what `plotChromPeaks()` draws, the overlay what `plot(type = \"XIC\")`\ndraws.\n\nThe panel reuses the peaks already held in the `detected_peaks` slot rather than\nintroducing a slot of its own, surfaced through the same derived-dataset\nmechanism the purity timeline uses. The frame handed over is a projection of\npeak geometry plus the join keys, because the render pipeline re-joins sample\nmetadata unconditionally and any column that already existed would come back\nsuffixed and break faceting. Which mode applies is decided from the finished\nplot rather than at composition time, so adding a host layer after the\nrectangles behaves the same as adding it before.\n\n`rt_range` and `mz_range` are new, the equivalents of `xlim` and `ylim` in\n`plotChromPeaks()`; a standalone panel has no host window to inherit. They reuse\nthe clamping the renderer already applied for the intensity map host, and are\nignored in overlay mode where the host supplies the window.\n\nVerified against the same faahKO experiment the xcms method uses: the standalone\npanel draws exactly the 87 rectangles `chromPeaks()` reports for that file. All\npre-existing vignette figures remain byte-identical.\n\nAlso trims the xcms cross-references and some explanatory prose from the layer\ndocumentation added in the previous commit.\n",
    "time": 1785745739
  },
